riscos-mesa: ETC1 textures in OpenGL ES, and GL_ARB_ES2_compatibility.

- GL_OES_compressed_ETC1_RGB8_texture (and EXT_compressed_ETC1_RGB8_sub_
  texture) in ES 1.1 and 2.0 contexts. ETC1 is the format the Raspberry
  Pi's own GPU uses, so ES code written for the Pi often ships its
  textures that way. The blocks are decoded when the texture is loaded
  (Mesa's own decoder) and kept as an ordinary RGB texture, so it draws
  through the fast textured paths instead of decoding every texel as it
  is sampled. glCompressedTexSubImage2D updates whole 4x4 blocks (or up
  to the image's edge); glTexSubImage2D and glCopyTexSubImage2D on an
  ETC1 texture are refused, as for any ES compressed texture.
- GL_ARB_ES2_compatibility in desktop GL contexts: OpenGL ES 2.0 shaders
  (#version 100, precision qualifiers), glClearDepthf, glDepthRangef,
  glGetShaderPrecisionFormat, GL_FIXED vertex attributes and the ES 2.0
  limits queries, so a renderer written for GLES2 also builds against
  desktop GL. The GL version stays 2.1.

Checked by tests/host-harness/mesa/render-etc1.c and glsl-es2compat.c.

